## 5. DYNAMIC KPI DATA ENTRY ENGINE

This is one of the most important components.

Every KPI may have a different formula and therefore requires different input variables.

Build a dynamic form-generation engine.

The system must automatically generate the relevant data-entry fields based on each approved KPI definition.

For example:

### Example A: Employee Turnover Rate

Formula:

Employee Turnover Rate (%) = (Number of Employees Who Left / Average Number of Employees) × 100

Required inputs:

- Number of employees who left.
- Average number of employees during the measurement period.

The user enters the numbers.

The platform automatically calculates the result.

### Example B: Absenteeism Rate

Formula:

Absenteeism Rate (%) = (Total Absence Days / Total Available Working Days) × 100

Required inputs:

- Total employee absence days.
- Total available working days.

### Example C: Time to Hire

Formula:

Time to Hire = Total Hiring Duration / Number of Completed Hires

Required inputs:

- Total hiring duration.
- Number of completed hires.

Important:

These examples are for demonstration purposes.

When actual KPI reference documents are imported, prioritize their approved definitions and formulas.

### Dynamic Form Requirements

Each input field should support:

- Field name
- Description
- Data type
- Measurement unit
- Required or optional status
- Minimum and maximum values
- Decimal precision
- Input frequency
- Validation rules
- Helpful instructions

Allow daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly, and annual data entry where relevant.

Store timestamps for every submission.

Allow authorized users to edit previously submitted values.

Maintain an audit trail of all changes.

Provide bulk entry through Excel or CSV.

Allow administrators to create custom data-entry templates.

Support data entry at organization, department, team, or employee-group level where appropriate.

